  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-BODY-003',
    number: 14,
    month: 1,
    question: 'Is white discharge in early pregnancy normal?',
    category: 'Body & Symptoms',
    applicability: 'All trimesters',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'A mild increase in thin, milky-white discharge is normal and common in early pregnancy. It is called leukorrhea, and it is actually a good sign, part of how your body protects against infection.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Rising estrogen and extra blood flow to the pelvic area lead your body to make more of this discharge, which helps keep the vagina healthy and guards against infections travelling upward. Normal leukorrhea is milky or clear, thin, and has only a mild smell or none at all, without itching or burning.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'For comfort you can wear unscented cotton panty liners and change them through the day, and avoid douching or scented washes, which upset the natural balance. It is worth checking if the discharge changes in a way that suggests infection, for example turning green, yellow or grey, becoming thick like cottage cheese, smelling foul or fishy, or coming with itching or burning.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Contact your doctor if the discharge changes colour, smells foul or fishy, or comes with itching or burning, as this can point to an infection that is easily treated.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'It can feel unglamorous and constant, but this discharge is largely your body quietly doing its protective job.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Thin, milky-white discharge is normal in early pregnancy.',
        'It is called leukorrhea and helps prevent infection.',
        'Normal discharge has no itching, burning or strong smell.',
        'Use cotton liners; avoid douching and scented products.',
        'See your doctor for green, yellow, cottage-cheese, foul-smelling or itchy discharge.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-BODY-001',
      'PREG-HEALTH-001',
      'PREG-BODY-002',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-CANI-DO-002',
    number: 15,
    month: 1,
    question: 'Can sex or orgasm cause a miscarriage in early pregnancy?',
    category: 'Exercise & Lifestyle (Can I > Do)',
    applicability: 'All trimesters',
    canI: 'Do',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'In a normal, uncomplicated pregnancy, sex and orgasm do not cause miscarriage. Your baby is well protected inside the womb, so intimacy is generally safe unless your doctor has advised otherwise.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'The baby is cushioned by the amniotic sac and the strong muscles of the womb, with a mucus plug sealing the cervix against infection. Orgasm and the prostaglandins in semen can cause mild, brief cramping afterwards, and a little spotting can happen because the cervix has extra blood flow, but neither harms the pregnancy. Most early miscarriages are caused by chromosomal differences, not by anything you did.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'If you feel comfortable and your pregnancy is uncomplicated, there is no need to avoid sex, and using a condom is sensible if there is any infection risk. There are a few situations where your doctor may advise pausing, such as a low-lying placenta, unexplained bleeding, or a history of preterm birth.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Check with your doctor before continuing sex if you have bleeding, a low-lying placenta, or a history of pregnancy loss or preterm birth.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'The fear that intimacy could harm the baby is extremely common and almost always unfounded; understanding why can take that worry off the table.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Sex and orgasm do not cause miscarriage in a normal pregnancy.',
        'The baby is protected by the womb, sac and mucus plug.',
        'Mild cramps or light spotting after sex can be normal.',
        'Use a condom if there is any infection risk.',
        'Check with your doctor if you have bleeding or specific conditions.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-CANI-EAT-011',
      'PREG-HEALTH-001',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-SCAN-021',
    number: 16,
    month: 1,
    question: 'What happens at my first doctor (antenatal) visit?',
    category: 'Scans & Appointments',
    applicability: 'Month 1 to 2',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Your first antenatal visit, ideally around 6 to 8 weeks, is the longest and most thorough of your pregnancy. Your doctor takes your history, does a basic check-up, arranges your first blood tests, and starts you on folic acid.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'This visit sets up your whole pregnancy. Your doctor asks about your health, past pregnancies and any conditions like thyroid, diabetes or high blood pressure, then checks your weight, height and blood pressure. They confirm your dates, often with an early scan, and order your booking blood tests. This is also when your care plan and visit schedule are set, and where you can ask anything on your mind.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'It helps to come prepared: carry any past medical records, a list of medicines you take, the date of your last period, and your questions written down. If you conceived through IVF or IUI, tell your doctor so they can plan accordingly.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Book this visit as soon as your test is positive, ideally within the first 8 weeks, so nothing important is missed.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'The first visit can feel test-heavy and a little overwhelming, but it is really the moment your care becomes personal and planned rather than guesswork.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Ideally at 6 to 8 weeks; the longest visit.',
        'Covers history, a check-up, your dates and folic acid.',
        'Booking blood tests are arranged.',
        'Bring records, a medicines list, your LMP date and questions.',
        'Mention IVF or IUI if relevant.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-TEST-002',
      'PREG-SCAN-020',
      'PREG-CANI-TAKE-001',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-TEST-002',
    number: 17,
    month: 1,
    question: 'What blood tests are done in early pregnancy?',
    category: 'Tests & Reports',
    applicability: 'Month 1 to 2',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Early in pregnancy your doctor orders a set of routine booking blood tests. These usually include a blood count, your blood group and Rh, thyroid, blood sugar, and screening for infections like HIV, hepatitis B and syphilis, along with a urine test.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Each test has a clear purpose. The blood count checks for anaemia, which is common in India and easily treated. Your blood group and Rh matter in case you need an injection later. Thyroid and blood sugar are checked because both affect pregnancy and are manageable when found early. Infection screening protects both you and your baby, since some infections can be treated to prevent passing them on. In India these are standard antenatal care, and many are free at government centres.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'There is usually nothing to prepare beyond attending the visit, and your doctor will explain any result that needs follow-up. Seeing a long list of tests is normal and is simply the foundation of good care, not a sign that anything is wrong.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Your doctor will explain any test that needs follow-up; ask if you are unsure what a result means.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'A long panel of blood tests can look alarming, but it is routine groundwork, and most come back reassuring.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Routine bloods include blood count, blood group and Rh, thyroid, blood sugar.',
        'Infection screening covers HIV, hepatitis B and syphilis.',
        'A urine test is done too.',
        'Most happen at your first visit.',
        'Many are available free at government centres.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-SCAN-021',
      'PREG-SCAN-024',
      'PREG-SCAN-028',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-BODY-004',
    number: 18,
    month: 1,
    question: 'How soon can morning sickness start, and what helps?',
    category: 'Symptoms',
    applicability: 'First trimester',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Morning sickness, meaning pregnancy nausea, most often starts around weeks 5 to 6 and usually eases by weeks 12 to 14. Despite the name, it can happen at any time of day, and simple steps often help you through the worst of it.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'The nausea is driven by the rapid rise in pregnancy hormones in the early weeks. It affects a large majority of women to some degree and, while unpleasant, is usually harmless and a sign your hormones are doing their job. For most, it fades as the first trimester ends.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Small, frequent meals rather than large ones, keeping something plain like a biscuit by your bed, staying hydrated, and ginger, in tea, water or a small piece, are among the most reliable comforts. Eating something before you get up, and avoiding smells that trigger you, can also help. If simple steps are not enough, your doctor can suggest pregnancy-safe options.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'See your doctor if you cannot keep fluids down, are losing weight, or the nausea is severe enough that you cannot manage daily life.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'There is an old idea that nausea must simply be endured; in truth, ordinary morning sickness usually passes, and there is no need to suffer in silence if it is severe.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Usually starts around weeks 5 to 6, eases by weeks 12 to 14.',
        'Can happen at any time of day.',
        'Small frequent meals, hydration and ginger often help.',
        'Eat something before getting up.',
        'Ask your doctor if it is severe.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-HEALTH-011',
      'PREG-BODY-001',
      'PREG-CANI-EAT-001',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-004',
    number: 19,
    month: 1,
    question: 'How is early pregnancy different after IVF?',
    category: 'High-Risk & Special Pregnancies',
    applicability: 'Month 1 to 2',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'Yes (fertility journey, gentler register)',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'After IVF, early pregnancy is confirmed with a beta hCG blood test about 9 to 14 days after your embryo transfer, and you usually stay under your fertility clinic\'s care until around 7 to 10 weeks before moving to your regular pregnancy doctor. The pregnancy itself develops much like any other.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'The beta hCG test measures the pregnancy hormone, and your clinic often repeats it to check the level is rising steadily, which is more reassuring than any single number. A first scan is typically done around 6 to 7 weeks to confirm the pregnancy and heartbeat. IVF pregnancies are biologically very similar to naturally conceived ones, but they are watched a little more closely, and once you move to your OB, dating switches to the usual last-period system.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'The two-week wait and early weeks can feel especially anxious after everything you have been through, which is completely understandable. It helps to follow your clinic\'s testing plan rather than relying on early home tests, and to keep taking any prescribed medicines exactly as advised until told otherwise.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Follow your fertility clinic\'s testing and medication plan closely, and tell your future pregnancy doctor about your IVF history so your care is tailored.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'After the effort of IVF, early pregnancy can carry extra worry; leaning on your clinic\'s guidance, and support when you need it, matters as much as the numbers.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Beta hCG test about 9 to 14 days after transfer confirms pregnancy.',
        'A rising level matters more than any single number.',
        'A first scan is usually around 6 to 7 weeks.',
        'You move to your OB around 7 to 10 weeks.',
        'The pregnancy develops much like any other, with closer monitoring.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
