  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-BODY-009',
    number: 27,
    month: 1,
    question: 'Why do I need to pee so often in early pregnancy?',
    category: 'Body & Symptoms',
    applicability: 'First trimester onward',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Needing to pee more often is one of the earliest and most common signs of pregnancy, and it is completely normal. It happens because your body makes more blood and your kidneys have more fluid to process.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'In early pregnancy your blood volume rises and hormones increase blood flow to the kidneys, so they produce more urine. A little later the growing womb also presses on the bladder. All of this means more trips to the bathroom, especially at night, and it is simply your body adapting.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'There is no need to cut back on fluids, as staying hydrated matters; you can, though, drink a little less in the hour or two before bed to reduce night-time trips. What is worth watching for is burning, pain or urgency when you pee, which can suggest an infection.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'See your doctor if peeing comes with burning, pain, or blood, as this can point to a UTI (urinary tract infection) that needs treatment.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Frequent bathroom trips are an early and lasting companion of pregnancy; they are a normal sign of your body doing its work.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Frequent urination is a normal early sign.',
        'Caused by more blood and busier kidneys.',
        'Later the womb presses on the bladder too.',
        'Keep drinking water, but ease off just before bed.',
        'See your doctor if peeing burns or hurts.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-CANI-DRINK-003',
      'PREG-BODY-001',
      'PREG-BODY-003',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-005',
    number: 28,
    month: 1,
    question: 'How do I know if I\'m expecting twins?',
    category: 'High-Risk & Special Pregnancies',
    applicability: 'From ~6 weeks',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'The only reliable way to know you are expecting twins is an ultrasound scan, which can often show two babies from around 6 to 7 weeks. Some women do notice stronger early symptoms, but these alone cannot confirm it.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Twin pregnancies often come with more of the hormone hCG (human chorionic gonadotropin), which can mean stronger nausea, more fatigue, faster weight gain, or a bump showing earlier. These signs are suggestive but not proof, since every pregnancy differs. A scan is what confirms the number of babies, and many twins are first seen at the routine 11 to 14 week scan. Twins are more likely with IVF (in vitro fertilisation) or other fertility treatment, a family history of non-identical twins, older maternal age, or a previous twin pregnancy.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'If you suspect twins, there is nothing special you need to do beyond attending your scans; your doctor will confirm and, if it is twins, arrange closer monitoring, since twin pregnancies carry a higher chance of complications. Many twin pregnancies go well with the right care.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Your scan will confirm whether it is twins; if it is, your doctor will set up more frequent monitoring.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Wondering "is it twins?" is one of pregnancy\'s most exciting early questions, and only the scan can truly answer it.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Only a scan reliably confirms twins, often from 6 to 7 weeks.',
        'Stronger symptoms can hint at it but cannot confirm.',
        'Higher chance with IVF, family history, older age or previous twins.',
        'Twins need closer monitoring.',
        'Many twin pregnancies go well.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-SCAN-020',
      'PREG-HEALTH-004',
      'PREG-BODY-004',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-CANI-DRINK-003',
    number: 29,
    month: 1,
    question: 'How much water should I drink in pregnancy?',
    category: 'Nutrition (Can I > Drink)',
    applicability: 'All trimesters',
    canI: 'Drink',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Aim for roughly 8 to 12 glasses of fluid a day in pregnancy, about 2 to 2.5 litres, a little more than before. All drinks count, and a simple check is that your urine stays a pale, clear colour.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Pregnancy raises your body\'s fluid needs, because you are making more blood, creating the fluid around your baby, and supporting the placenta. Staying well hydrated also eases common early complaints like constipation, tiredness and headaches, and helps prevent urinary tract infections. In India\'s heat, and if you are being sick with morning sickness, you can lose fluid faster and need to top up more.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Sip steadily through the day rather than large amounts at once, keep a bottle with you, and use milk, buttermilk, coconut water, soups and water-rich fruits to add to your fluids. Keep caffeine under the usual limit, and go easy on very sugary drinks.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Tell your doctor if you cannot keep fluids down, or have signs of dehydration like very dark urine, dizziness or a dry mouth that does not improve.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Hydration is one of the simplest, most underrated things you can do for how you feel day to day in pregnancy.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Aim for about 8 to 12 glasses (2 to 2.5 litres) a day.',
        'All fluids count.',
        'Pale, clear urine is a good sign.',
        'It helps with constipation, tiredness and headaches.',
        'Sip steadily, especially in the heat or with morning sickness.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-BODY-005',
      'PREG-BODY-006',
      'PREG-CANI-DRINK-001',
    ],
