  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-EMOTION-001',
    number: 30,
    month: 1,
    question: 'Is it normal to feel anxious or unsure after finding out I\'m pregnant?',
    category: 'Emotional Wellbeing',
    applicability: 'First trimester',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'Yes (mental health, gentle register; routes to a soft support/counsellor path, not product content)',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'It is completely normal to feel a mix of emotions after finding out you are pregnant, including joy, worry, and even moments of sadness or uncertainty. Wanting a pregnancy and still feeling anxious about it are not contradictions.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Early pregnancy brings a flood of hormones and a huge life change at once, so mixed and shifting feelings are common and understandable. Some women feel an immediate bond, others do not for a while, and both are normal. Everyday worries are one thing; when anxiety or low mood becomes constant, hard to control, or starts interfering with daily life, that is a sign to reach out for support.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Small things can help: naming what you feel, leaning on someone you trust, keeping gentle routines, rest and movement, and giving yourself permission to feel more than one thing at once. You do not have to carry heavy feelings alone, and telling your doctor is a good and ordinary step.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Speak to your doctor if worry or low mood feels constant, hard to control, or is affecting your daily life, so you can get the right support.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'You are allowed to want this deeply and still find it overwhelming; both can be true, and neither makes you any less ready to be a parent.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Mixed feelings in early pregnancy are normal.',
        'You can want a pregnancy and still feel anxious.',
        'Bonding may be immediate or take time.',
        'Reach out if worry or low mood is constant or disruptive.',
        'Sharing with your doctor or someone you trust helps.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-GEN-003',
      'PREG-BODY-005',
      'PREG-BODY-001',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-GS-001',
    number: 31,
    month: 1,
    question: 'What is Garbh Sanskar?',
    category: 'Garbh Sanskar & Bonding',
    applicability: 'All trimesters',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Garbh Sanskar is a traditional Indian practice, rooted in Ayurveda, whose name means "nurturing or educating the baby in the womb." It rests on a sound idea: that what a mother eats, feels and experiences in pregnancy reaches her growing baby and helps shape how the baby develops.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'Mother and baby are closely connected through the placenta, so much of what the mother experiences genuinely reaches the baby, through three pathways science understands well. Nutrition: your baby\'s brain and body are built from the nutrients that cross the placenta, so a nourishing diet supplies the raw materials for healthy growth, above all in the early weeks. Calm: sustained high stress sends more of the stress hormone cortisol to the baby, influencing the developing brain areas for emotion, which is why a mother\'s calm helps steady the baby\'s foundation, not just her own comfort. The senses: from around mid-pregnancy your baby begins to hear, coming to know your voice and heartbeat, so a newborn recognises and is soothed by them. Garbh Sanskar\'s habits, calm, sattvic food, soothing music and talking to your baby, map onto these real pathways. It cannot promise a "more intelligent" child, that is traditional belief, but that it supports the best environment for your baby is entirely reasonable.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Treat it as a comforting daily rhythm, not strict rules. Take the parts that bring peace and let go of any pressure. It sits alongside your medical care, never replacing your scans, tests or doctor\'s advice.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Garbh Sanskar complements antenatal care; keep up your scans and check-ups, and ask before starting prenatal yoga.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'At heart, it reflects what science increasingly agrees: a calm, well-nourished, loving pregnancy is a real gift to your baby.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Garbh Sanskar means "nurturing the baby in the womb," rooted in Ayurveda.',
        'Much of what you eat, feel and experience reaches your baby.',
        'Nutrition builds the brain, calm lowers stress hormones reaching the baby, and your voice becomes familiar.',
        'It cannot promise a fixed outcome, but supports the best environment for development.',
        'It complements, and never replaces, medical care.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-GS-002',
      'PREG-BABY-003',
      'PREG-CANI-EAT-001',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-GS-002',
    number: 32,
    month: 1,
    question: 'When should I start Garbh Sanskar, and how do I do it at home?',
    category: 'Garbh Sanskar & Bonding',
    applicability: 'From Month 1',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'You can start Garbh Sanskar as soon as you know you are pregnant, and some families even begin before conception. There is no single "correct" time, and the first trimester simply focuses on gentle foundations.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Because the practice is about the mother\'s overall wellbeing, early habits centre on staying calm, eating well and resting, rather than anything elaborate. Traditional teaching values starting early, but the practice is meant to run gently through all nine months, adapting as pregnancy progresses. In the first trimester, when nausea and tiredness are common, keeping things simple matters more than doing everything.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'A realistic home routine might be a few quiet minutes each morning with positive thoughts or prayer, some calming music while you rest or work, reading something uplifting, a fresh home-cooked sattvic meal, and gentle prenatal yoga once your doctor okays it. Talking to your baby can start whenever you like, even though hearing develops later in pregnancy; it is really about your bond. Pick two or three of these and let them fit around how you feel.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Check with your doctor before starting prenatal yoga or any new exercise, especially in the first trimester.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'There is no need to begin perfectly or all at once; even one small calming habit is a meaningful start.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Start whenever you learn you are pregnant, or earlier.',
        'The first trimester keeps things gentle.',
        'A simple daily rhythm works better than doing everything.',
        'Talking to baby is about bonding; hearing comes later.',
        'Check with your doctor before starting yoga.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-GS-001',
      'PREG-BABY-003',
      'PREG-CANI-EAT-001',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-BABY-003',
    number: 33,
    month: 1,
    question: 'How can I support my baby\'s brain development during pregnancy?',
    category: 'Baby Development / Nutrition',
    applicability: 'All trimesters',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'There is a great deal you can do to give your baby\'s brain the best start, and the early weeks matter most, since this is when the brain first forms. The main levers are good nutrition, a calm and healthy you, and avoiding harmful exposures.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'Your baby\'s brain is built from what reaches it through you. Certain nutrients are the building blocks: folate (folic acid), iron, iodine, choline, omega-3 fats such as DHA (docosahexaenoic acid), vitamin B12 and vitamin D; getting enough lays down healthy structure, while alcohol and smoking can interfere, which is why avoiding them protects development. Your calm matters too: sustained high stress raises cortisol that reaches the baby and shapes the developing stress and emotion centres, so staying supported benefits your baby, not only you. And from mid-pregnancy your baby can hear, so your voice, gentle music and being read to become early experiences that help build hearing and language, which is why newborns already recognise their mother\'s voice. No one can honestly promise a specific level of cleverness, but you can genuinely stack the odds in your baby\'s favour from every helpful angle.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Take your folic acid and any supplements your doctor advises, eat iron-rich foods like dals and leafy greens, use iodised salt, and include omega-3 sources such as walnuts, flaxseed or low-mercury fish. Keeping your own mind calm and engaged, reading, creativity, music, gentle activity, helps too, and later becomes something you share with your baby by talking or singing to your bump.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Ask which supplements are right for you, especially if vegetarian, as vitamin B12 and omega-3 can need attention.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'The most meaningful things are wonderfully ordinary: nourish yourself, stay calm, and share your world with your baby.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'The early weeks matter most, as the brain begins forming then.',
        'Building blocks: folate, iron, iodine, choline, omega-3, B12, vitamin D.',
        'Avoiding alcohol and smoking protects development.',
        'Staying calm helps, since your stress reaches the baby.',
        'From mid-pregnancy, your voice, music and reading become real experiences for your baby.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-GS-001',
      'PREG-CANI-TAKE-001',
      'PREG-CANI-EAT-001',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-EMOTION-002',
    number: 34,
    month: 1,
    question: 'Are there prayers or duas I can say during pregnancy?',
    category: 'Emotional Wellbeing',
    applicability: 'All trimesters',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'Yes (faith and emotional wellbeing; handled inclusively)',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Yes, prayers, duas, mantras or any spiritual practice from your faith are welcome during pregnancy, and many families find them deeply comforting. They are a personal choice, and their main benefit is the calm and connection they bring.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Pregnancy can stir up worry, and turning to prayer, recitation or quiet reflection can ease anxiety and create a sense of peace and hope. Across traditions, whether it is duas, shlokas, mantras, gurbani or silent prayer, the shared thread is comfort, gratitude and bonding with your baby. This calming effect is real and valuable for your wellbeing, even though prayer is not a substitute for medical care.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'You might weave a short prayer or recitation into your day, at a fixed time or whenever you need steadying. There is no need to follow anyone else\'s exact practice; choose what feels meaningful to you and your family. If certain rituals involve fasting or physical strain, just check that part with your doctor.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Spiritual practice sits alongside your medical care; check with your doctor only if a ritual involves fasting or physical strain.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Whatever form it takes, this quiet time is as much a gift to you as to your baby, a few moments of calm in a big season of change.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Prayers, duas and mantras from any faith are welcome.',
        'Their main benefit is calm and connection.',
        'Choose what feels meaningful to you.',
        'Not a replacement for medical care.',
        'Check fasting or strenuous rituals with your doctor.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-GS-001',
      'PREG-EMOTION-001',
      'PREG-CANI-DO-003',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-006',
    number: 35,
    month: 1,
    question: 'What is anemia in pregnancy and how do I prevent it?',
    category: 'Health & Medical / Nutrition',
    applicability: 'All trimesters (screened Month 1 to 2)',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Anemia means your blood has too little haemoglobin to carry oxygen well, and it is very common in Indian pregnancies, affecting around half of women. The good news is it is usually easy to prevent and treat.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Pregnancy sharply increases your iron needs, and if intake falls short, iron-deficiency anemia results; low folate or vitamin B12 can also cause it. In India, largely plant-based diets provide iron that is less easily absorbed, which adds to the risk. Anemia is measured by a haemoglobin (Hb) blood test, one reason it is checked at your first antenatal visit; a level below 11 grams per decilitre is considered anemia. Common signs are tiredness, breathlessness, palpitations, and looking pale.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Eat iron-rich foods like dals, green leafy vegetables, jaggery, beetroot, dates and, if you eat them, egg, meat or fish. Pair them with vitamin C, for example a squeeze of lemon or some amla, to boost absorption, and avoid tea or coffee right with meals, as they reduce it. Take the iron and folic acid tablets your doctor prescribes, which are also available free at government health centres.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Your doctor will check your haemoglobin and prescribe iron if needed; tell them if tablets upset your stomach so the dose can be adjusted.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Anemia is so common in India that finding it is routine rather than alarming, and it usually responds well once treated.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Anemia means too little haemoglobin to carry oxygen.',
        'It affects about half of Indian pregnancies.',
        'It is checked by an Hb test at your first visit.',
        'Eat iron-rich foods with vitamin C; avoid tea and coffee at meals.',
        'Take prescribed iron and folic acid, free at government centres.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-TEST-002',
      'PREG-CANI-EAT-001',
      'PREG-BODY-005',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-BODY-010',
    number: 36,
    month: 1,
    question: 'Are breast changes normal in early pregnancy?',
    category: 'Body & Symptoms',
    applicability: 'First trimester onward',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Yes, breast changes are one of the earliest and most common signs of pregnancy. Tenderness, tingling, fullness, and darkening around the nipples are all normal and driven by rising hormones.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Early in pregnancy, hormones like estrogen and progesterone rise and blood flow to the breasts increases, as your body begins, very early, to prepare for feeding. This can make breasts feel sore, swollen or heavier, with the areola (the area around the nipple) darkening and veins becoming more visible. The soreness is often strongest in the first weeks and usually eases as your body adjusts.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'A well-fitting, supportive bra, including a soft one for sleep, makes a real difference, as does a warm or cool compress for comfort. If you feel you need any pain relief, check with your doctor first. These changes are simply a sign your body is doing exactly what it should.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Mention any breast lump that feels new or distinct, or one-sided redness and pain, so your doctor can check it.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Tender, changing breasts can be uncomfortable, but they are among the body\'s quiet, early signs of getting ready.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Breast changes are a common early sign.',
        'Tenderness, tingling, fullness and darker areolas are normal.',
        'Caused by hormones and increased blood flow.',
        'A supportive bra and compresses help.',
        'For any pain relief, check with your doctor first.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-BODY-001',
      'PREG-BODY-002',
      'PREG-CANI-TAKE-002',
