    month: 1,
    question: 'Are headaches normal in early pregnancy?',
    category: 'Body & Symptoms',
    applicability: 'First trimester',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Headaches are common in early pregnancy and are usually harmless. They are linked to hormonal changes, increased blood volume, and sometimes dehydration or missing meals.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'The surge of pregnancy hormones and the rise in blood volume can both trigger headaches, and nausea that puts you off food and fluids can add dehydration or low blood sugar to the mix. Cutting down caffeine suddenly can also bring them on. For most women these headaches are a passing discomfort of the early weeks.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Simple steps help most: drink enough water, eat small regular meals, rest, and keep a steady sleep routine. If you feel you need any medicine for the pain, check with your doctor first. Reducing caffeine gradually rather than all at once can prevent withdrawal headaches.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'See your doctor promptly if a headache is severe or persistent, or comes with blurred vision, swelling, or upper-tummy pain, as these need checking.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Ordinary early-pregnancy headaches are usually about hydration, rest and hormones, and small habits often settle them.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Headaches are common and usually harmless early on.',
        'Caused by hormones, blood volume, dehydration or skipped meals.',
        'Hydration, regular meals, rest and sleep help.',
        'For any medicine, get your doctor\'s guidance.',
        'Get severe headaches with vision changes or swelling checked.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
