    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Colouring your hair is generally considered safe in pregnancy, especially after the first trimester, because your scalp absorbs only a tiny amount of the chemicals. Most everyday cosmetics are fine too, with a few sensible precautions.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Studies, including of hairdressers with heavy exposure, have not shown a consistent link between hair dye and harm to the baby, and absorption through a healthy scalp is minimal. Many women still prefer to wait until the second trimester for extra peace of mind, since the first trimester is when the baby\'s organs are forming. Strong chemical fumes can also worsen early-pregnancy nausea.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'If you colour your hair, scalp-free methods like highlights, semi-permanent dyes, or pure henna (not "black henna," which can contain harsh chemicals) lower exposure further. Work in a well-ventilated room, wear gloves, do a patch test, rinse well, and avoid dyeing eyebrows or eyelashes. For cosmetics, most are fine; it is worth avoiding high-strength retinoid (vitamin A) creams, which are better paused in pregnancy.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
