    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'A metallic taste in the mouth and a sharper sense of smell are common, if odd, early pregnancy symptoms. Both are driven mainly by rising hormones, and both usually fade by the second trimester.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'The metallic or "coin in the mouth" taste is called dysgeusia, and the heightened smell is sometimes called "pregnancy nose"; both are linked to the surge in estrogen, which strongly influences taste and smell, along with increased blood flow. They can start very early, sometimes within the first few weeks, and often peak between weeks five and nine before easing as hormones settle. They are harmless, even if they make food and everyday smells feel strange.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'A few simple things can help: sip water with lemon, or suck on citrus or sugar-free mints, eat cold or crunchy foods, keep up gentle oral care including brushing your tongue, and rinse with a little salt and baking soda in warm water before meals. If your prenatal supplement seems to worsen the taste, mention it, as switching can sometimes help.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Mention it to your doctor if it comes with severe nausea, vomiting or fever, so anything else can be ruled out.',
