    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Routine dental care, including check-ups, cleanings and fillings, is safe and even encouraged during pregnancy, and dental X-rays are considered safe when done with proper shielding. Looking after your teeth and gums matters, because pregnancy hormones can make gums more prone to swelling and bleeding.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Untreated dental problems and gum infection can be more of a risk than the treatment itself, so keeping up dental care protects you. Dental X-rays use a very small dose of radiation aimed at the mouth, well away from your abdomen, and dentists add a lead apron and thyroid collar for extra protection. Many dentists prefer to schedule non-urgent work in the second trimester for comfort, while urgent care can be done safely at any stage.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Do tell your dentist you are pregnant and how many weeks along you are, so they can plan timing and take the usual precautions. Keep up gentle brushing and flossing, and do not put off treating pain or infection out of worry. For any medicines used during dental work, your dentist and doctor will choose options suitable for pregnancy.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Tell your dentist you are pregnant so treatment can be timed and shielded, and do not delay care for pain or infection.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Healthy gums are part of a healthy pregnancy, so dental care is something to keep up, not avoid.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Routine dental care is safe and encouraged.',
        'Dental X-rays are safe with a lead apron and thyroid collar.',
