        'If you have PCOS (polycystic ovary syndrome), you can absolutely have a healthy pregnancy; it simply means your pregnancy is watched a little more closely. PCOS is linked with some higher risks, which good care is designed to catch early and manage.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'PCOS involves hormonal and insulin-related differences that can make conceiving harder and are associated with a somewhat higher chance of early miscarriage, gestational diabetes and high blood pressure in pregnancy. Knowing this simply means your doctor keeps a closer eye on things like your blood sugar and blood pressure, so anything can be picked up and managed promptly. Many women with PCOS go on to have smooth pregnancies and healthy babies.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Let your doctor know about your PCOS early so your care can be planned, including timely glucose testing later in pregnancy. Keeping active within what your doctor advises and eating balanced meals supports steadier blood sugar. If you take any medication for PCOS, your doctor will tell you whether and how to continue it in pregnancy.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Tell your doctor about your PCOS early so monitoring can be planned, and let them guide any medication and testing.',
