    id: 'PREG-BODY-016',
    number: 50,
    month: 1,
    question: 'Why do I look bloated or "showing" already in early pregnancy?',
    category: 'Body & Symptoms',
    applicability: 'First trimester',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'If you feel like you are already showing in the first weeks, it is almost always bloating rather than a baby bump. A real bump usually does not appear until the second trimester.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Early pregnancy hormones slow digestion and cause bloating, which can make your tummy feel fuller or look a little rounder, especially by evening. The womb itself is still low in the pelvis in the first trimester and is not yet big enough to show. Most first-time mothers begin to show somewhere around the middle of pregnancy, while those who have been pregnant before, or who are carrying twins, may show a little earlier, and body type makes a natural difference too.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'There is no "right" time to show, so try not to compare your bump, or lack of one, with anyone else\'s. For early bloating, eating smaller and slower, staying hydrated and gentle movement can ease the puffy feeling, and comfortable, loose clothing helps long before you need maternity wear.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'There is no need to see your doctor about the timing of your bump; do mention any sudden, painful swelling of your tummy, which is different from ordinary bloating.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Every bump arrives on its own schedule, so an early or late one says nothing about how your pregnancy is going.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Early "showing" is usually bloating, not a bump.',
        'The real bump typically appears in the second trimester.',
        'First-time mothers often show later, second-time or twins earlier.',
        'Body type makes a natural difference.',
        'Smaller meals, hydration and loose clothing help.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-BODY-006',
      'PREG-CANI-DRINK-003',
      'PREG-BODY-001',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-BABY-004',
    number: 51,
    month: 1,
    question: 'How does a pregnancy actually begin?',
    category: 'Baby Development (Wonder)',
    applicability: 'Month 1',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'A pregnancy begins when a sperm meets and fertilises an egg, usually in the fallopian tube, around the time of ovulation. That single fertilised cell then starts dividing as it travels toward the womb.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'This tiny moment sets everything in motion. The fertilised egg, now called a zygote, divides again and again over several days into a small ball of cells as it makes its way to the womb. From this one cell come both your baby and the placenta that will support it. Because pregnancy is dated from your last period, you are already considered around two weeks pregnant by the time conception actually happens.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'There is nothing you need to do at this stage, and most women feel nothing when it happens. Since the brain and other organs begin forming very soon after, this is exactly why folic acid and a nourishing diet, ideally begun early or even before conceiving, matter so much.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'No action is needed at conception itself; your care begins once a test is positive and you book your first visit.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'It is quietly astonishing that everything your baby will become starts from a single cell smaller than a full stop.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Pregnancy begins when sperm fertilises an egg, usually in the fallopian tube.',
        'The fertilised cell divides as it travels to the womb.',
        'Both baby and placenta grow from it.',
        'You are dated about 2 weeks pregnant by the time it happens.',
        'Folic acid started early matters.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-GEN-002',
      'PREG-BABY-005',
      'PREG-CANI-TAKE-001',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-BABY-005',
    number: 52,
    month: 1,
    question: 'What is implantation?',
    category: 'Baby Development (Wonder)',
    applicability: 'Month 1',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Implantation is when the tiny ball of cells from the fertilised egg settles into the lining of your womb, usually about seven to ten days after conception. This is the quiet moment a pregnancy truly takes root.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'Until it implants, the early embryo is simply travelling and dividing. Once it burrows into the nourishing womb lining, it can begin drawing what it needs to grow, and your body starts producing the pregnancy hormone hCG (human chorionic gonadotropin), which is what a pregnancy test detects. The outer cells that anchor it go on to form the placenta. Some women notice light spotting or mild cramps around this time, while many notice nothing at all.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'There is nothing you need to do, and feeling no symptoms is completely normal. If you see light pink or brown spotting around when your period was due, this can be implantation rather than a period, though only a test taken from your missed period can confirm it.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'No action is needed for implantation itself; simply test from your missed period and book your first visit if positive.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'It is one of pregnancy\'s most important milestones, and yet it usually passes completely unnoticed.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Implantation is when the embryo settles into the womb lining, about 7 to 10 days after conception.',
        'It lets the pregnancy draw nourishment and start making hCG.',
        'The anchoring cells become the placenta.',
        'Light spotting or nothing at all are both normal.',
        'Test from your missed period.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-BABY-004',
      'PREG-BODY-013',
      'PREG-TEST-001',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-BABY-006',
    number: 53,
    month: 1,
    question: 'What is the placenta and what does it do?',
    category: 'Baby Development (Foundational)',
    applicability: 'All trimesters',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'The placenta is a remarkable temporary organ that forms in pregnancy to nourish and protect your baby. It acts as your baby\'s lifeline, doing the work of the lungs, kidneys and digestive system until birth.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'The placenta grows from the same early cells as your baby and attaches to the wall of your womb, connecting to your baby through the umbilical cord. Through it, oxygen, nutrients and water pass from you to your baby, while waste passes back to be cleared by your body. It also makes hormones that keep the pregnancy going. It develops over the first few months and keeps working until your baby is born.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'You do not need to do anything special for the placenta; a nourishing diet, staying away from alcohol and smoking, and attending your scans all support it. Your doctor will check its position and health on your scans as a routine part of care.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Your scans check the placenta\'s position and health; there is nothing extra you need to do.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'The placenta is one of the few organs your body builds and then lets go of, entirely in the service of your baby.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'The placenta is a temporary organ that nourishes and protects your baby.',
        'It does the work of the lungs, kidneys and digestion.',
        'It passes oxygen and nutrients in, and waste out.',
        'It also makes pregnancy hormones.',
        'Scans check its position and health.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-BABY-007',
      'PREG-BABY-008',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-BABY-007',
    number: 54,
    month: 1,
    question: 'What is the umbilical cord?',
    category: 'Baby Development (Foundational)',
    applicability: 'All trimesters',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'The umbilical cord is the flexible, rope-like link between your baby and the placenta. It is your baby\'s supply line, carrying oxygen and nutrients in, and waste away.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'The cord usually contains three blood vessels, two arteries and one vein, cushioned by a soft, jelly-like tissue that protects them as your baby moves. Blood flows along it in both directions: nourishing blood from the placenta to your baby, and blood carrying waste back to the placenta for your body to clear. It forms in the early weeks and grows with your baby, allowing plenty of room to move.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'You do not need to do anything for the cord; it works on its own, and your doctor may look at it on scans as part of routine checks. After birth, the cord is clamped and cut, painlessly for your baby, leaving the small stump that becomes the navel.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'The cord is checked as part of routine scans; there is nothing you need to do.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'For nine months, this single cord quietly delivers everything your baby needs, and then its job is simply done.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'The cord connects your baby to the placenta.',
        'It usually has two arteries and one vein, cushioned by protective jelly.',
        'It carries oxygen and nutrients in, and waste out.',
        'It grows with your baby.',
        'It is painlessly cut after birth to form the navel.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-BABY-006',
      'PREG-BABY-008',
      'PREG-BABY-001',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-BABY-008',
    number: 55,
    month: 1,
    question: 'What is amniotic fluid?',
    category: 'Baby Development (Foundational)',
    applicability: 'All trimesters',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Amniotic fluid is the clear liquid that surrounds and cushions your baby inside a protective sac, often called the "waters." It lets your baby float, move and grow safely.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'Soon after implantation, membranes form a sac around your baby that fills with this fluid. It does several important jobs: it cushions your baby from bumps, keeps a steady, warm temperature, lets your baby move freely so muscles and bones develop, and even helps the lungs and digestive system practise and mature as your baby swallows and breathes it in and out. The amount is carefully balanced by your body and increases as your baby grows.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'You do not need to manage the fluid; staying well hydrated supports healthy levels. Your doctor checks the amount on your scans, as too little or too much can occasionally need attention, though most pregnancies are perfectly balanced.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Your scans check the fluid level; staying well hydrated helps, and your doctor will guide you if anything needs attention.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'The gentle pool your baby floats in is doing far more than cushioning, it is a training ground for life outside.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Amniotic fluid cushions and protects your baby in a sac.',
        'It keeps a steady temperature and lets your baby move.',
        'It helps the lungs and gut practise and mature.',
        'The amount is balanced by your body and checked on scans.',
        'Hydration supports healthy levels.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-BABY-006',
      'PREG-BABY-007',
      'PREG-CANI-DRINK-003',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-BODY-017',
    number: 56,
    month: 1,
    question: 'Are bleeding gums normal in pregnancy?',
    category: 'Body & Symptoms',
    applicability: 'All trimesters',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Sore or bleeding gums are common in pregnancy and usually nothing to worry about. Pregnancy hormones make the gums more sensitive and prone to swelling and bleeding, sometimes called pregnancy gingivitis.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Rising hormones increase blood flow to the gums and make them react more strongly to the plaque on your teeth, so they can look puffy and bleed a little when you brush. It is common and usually settles after pregnancy, but caring for your teeth and gums matters, because healthy gums are part of a healthy pregnancy.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Keep brushing gently twice a day with a soft brush, floss carefully, and rinse with a little warm salt water if your gums feel sore. Do not skip brushing because of the bleeding, as that makes it worse. If nausea makes brushing hard, a milder-flavoured toothpaste can help, and routine dental check-ups and cleanings are safe to keep up.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'See your dentist if your gums are very swollen, painful, or bleed a lot, and keep up routine dental check-ups.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Tender gums are a small, passing part of pregnancy, and gentle daily care handles them well.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Sore, bleeding gums are common and usually harmless.',
        'Caused by hormones making gums more sensitive.',
        'Keep brushing gently and floss.',
        'Warm salt-water rinses soothe.',
        'Routine dental care is safe and worthwhile.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-CANI-DO-006',
      'PREG-BODY-001',
      'PREG-BODY-004',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-BODY-018',
    number: 57,
    month: 1,
    question: 'Is a stuffy or bleeding nose normal in pregnancy?',
    category: 'Body & Symptoms',
    applicability: 'All trimesters',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'A stuffy, runny or occasionally bleeding nose is common in pregnancy and usually harmless. It is caused by rising hormones and increased blood flow to the lining of your nose, sometimes called pregnancy rhinitis.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Pregnancy hormones and the extra blood circulating in your body make the delicate membranes inside your nose swell and produce more mucus, leaving you congested even without a cold. The same richer blood supply makes tiny nasal blood vessels more likely to break, so occasional light nosebleeds are common. It usually eases after pregnancy.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Simple measures help: keep well hydrated, use a humidifier or saline nasal drops or sprays, and blow your nose gently. For a nosebleed, sit up, lean slightly forward, and pinch the soft part of your nose for a few minutes. Avoid using any decongestant spray or medicine unless your doctor advises it.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'See your doctor if nosebleeds are heavy, frequent or hard to stop, or if congestion comes with fever or facial pain.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'A blocked or occasionally bleeding nose is an unglamorous but harmless companion of pregnancy for many women.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'A stuffy or lightly bleeding nose is common and harmless.',
        'Caused by hormones and increased blood flow (pregnancy rhinitis).',
        'Hydration, saline drops and a humidifier help.',
        'Pinch and lean forward for a nosebleed.',
        'Check with your doctor before any medicine.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-HEALTH-009',
      'PREG-BODY-001',
      'PREG-CANI-DRINK-003',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-009',
    number: 58,
    month: 1,
    question: 'What can I do for a cold, cough or fever in pregnancy?',
    category: 'Health & Medical',
    applicability: 'All trimesters',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'A common cold or cough in pregnancy is usually uncomfortable rather than dangerous, and it tends to pass in about a week with rest and fluids. A fever, though, is worth taking seriously, especially in early pregnancy.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Your immune system works a little differently in pregnancy, so colds and coughs are common, and most are caused by ordinary viruses that do not harm your baby. A high temperature is different, because a significant fever in the first weeks has been linked to a higher risk of problems as the baby\'s brain and spine form, so it should not be left unchecked.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'For a cold or cough, rest, drink plenty of fluids, and try steam inhalation, warm salt-water gargles, and honey in warm water or tea. Use a humidifier or saline nasal drops for a blocked nose. Importantly, do not take any medicine, including over-the-counter cold or fever remedies, without your doctor\'s guidance.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Contact your doctor for any fever, for symptoms that do not improve in a few days, or for trouble breathing, chest pain or being unable to keep fluids down, and before taking any medicine.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Most coughs and colds simply need rest and patience, while a fever is the one sign worth checking promptly.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Colds and coughs are usually harmless and pass with rest and fluids.',
        'Steam, gargles and honey soothe symptoms.',
        'A fever, especially early on, should be checked.',
        'Do not take any medicine without your doctor\'s guidance.',
        'Watch for breathing trouble or dehydration.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-CANI-DO-007',
      'PREG-BODY-018',
      'PREG-HEALTH-010',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-CANI-DO-008',
    number: 59,
    month: 1,
    question: 'Can I keep working and lifting during pregnancy?',
    category: 'Work & Travel (Can I > Do)',
    applicability: 'All trimesters',
    canI: 'Do',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Most women can safely keep working through pregnancy, including into the later months. The main things to be sensible about are heavy lifting, long periods of standing, and overexertion.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'For a healthy, uncomplicated pregnancy, ordinary work and everyday activity are fine and even good for you. Heavy manual lifting and long hours on your feet have been linked in some studies to a higher chance of complications, so easing off these is a sensible precaution rather than a strict ban. Pregnancy hormones also loosen your joints and shift your balance, which makes careful movement more important.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Lift with care, bending at your knees and keeping loads close, and ask for help with anything heavy. If you stand a lot, take breaks, use a stool or shift your weight, wear comfortable footwear, and put your feet up when you can. Stay hydrated, rest when tired, and speak to your doctor about your specific job if it involves heavy lifting, night shifts, long standing or hazardous conditions.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Talk to your doctor about your job if it involves heavy lifting, long standing, night shifts or hazards, especially if your pregnancy is high-risk.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Working through pregnancy is normal and healthy for most women; a few small adjustments keep it comfortable.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Most women can safely keep working through pregnancy.',
        'Avoid heavy lifting and long standing, and don\'t overexert.',
        'Lift with your knees and ask for help.',
        'Take breaks, hydrate and rest.',
        'Discuss a physically demanding job with your doctor.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-CANI-DO-001',
      'PREG-BODY-005',
      'PREG-CANI-EAT-001',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-010',
    number: 60,
    month: 1,
    question: 'Which vaccines do I need in pregnancy?',
    category: 'Health & Medical',
    applicability: 'All trimesters',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Some vaccines are recommended in every pregnancy to protect you and your baby, mainly tetanus-containing vaccines and the flu (influenza) vaccine. A few others, the live vaccines, are avoided during pregnancy.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.whyMatters, '', [
        'In India, tetanus vaccination, as TT (tetanus toxoid) or the combined Td or Tdap, is a standard, important part of antenatal care, given in every pregnancy to protect mother and baby from tetanus, and Tdap also protects the newborn against whooping cough. The inactivated flu vaccine is safe at any stage and guards against a flu that can be more serious in pregnancy. These are inactivated vaccines, meaning they cannot cause the illness. Live vaccines, such as MMR (measles, mumps, rubella) and chickenpox, are avoided in pregnancy and given before or after instead.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Your doctor will advise which vaccines you need and when, and will keep a record in your pregnancy notes. It helps to mention your vaccination history and any allergies. Family members who will care for the baby may also be advised to be up to date, to protect your newborn.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Your doctor will recommend the right vaccines and timing for you; share your vaccination history and any allergies.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'A couple of well-timed vaccines are among the simplest ways to protect both you and your baby, before and after birth.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Tetanus-containing vaccines and the inactivated flu vaccine are recommended in every pregnancy.',
        'In India, tetanus (TT/Td/Tdap) is routine antenatal care.',
        'Tdap also protects the newborn from whooping cough.',
        'Inactivated vaccines cannot cause illness.',
        'Live vaccines like MMR and chickenpox are avoided in pregnancy.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-SCAN-021',
      'PREG-HEALTH-009',
      'PREG-TEST-002',
    ],
  ),
